WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.140
You wouldn't think it'd take very much to
convince someone to direct the latest installment

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.550
in the hugely successful Batman film franchise.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:14.469
But as it turns out, there was one huge reason
Matt Reeves didn't want to take the job — keep

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.839
watching to find out what it was.

00:00:17.839 --> 00:00:19.970
“And here…we…go.”

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:25.619
The latest reboot featuring DC Comics' famed
Caped Crusader, "The Batman," is hitting theaters

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:31.130
worldwide, finally completing a monumental
task undertaken by writer-director Matt Reeves.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:36.410
"The Batman," of course, has undergone different
iterations during its arduous production cycle,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.399
which began with filmmaker Zack Snyder's Batman
choice, Ben Affleck, not only starring in

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:44.520
the title role but also writing and directing
the film.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.310
When Affleck left his "Batman" project behind
over personal reasons, the film's studio,

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:53.440
Warner Bros., had to figure out who could
take charge of the project next.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.510
Fostered by celebrated filmmaker J.J.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.059
Abrams on the writer-director's WB series
"Felicity," Matt Reeves' film breakthrough

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:04.489
came at the helm of the Abrams-produced found-footage
monster movie "Cloverfield."

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:09.430
The doors of opportunity swung wide open for
Reeves after the success of that film, which

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:15.620
earned $172 million at the worldwide box office
against a $25 million budget.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.690
This landed the filmmaker such high-profile
gigs as 20th Century Films' "Rise of the Planet

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.610
of the Apes" sequels "Dawn of the Planet of
the Apes" and "War for the Planet of the Apes"

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.560
in 2014 and 2017, respectively.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.950
Suddenly, Reeves was calling his own shots
as both writer and director of the last two

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.890
films in the "Apes" trilogy, which was clearly
the right move for the studio to make, seeing

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.960
how the entire trilogy is widely considered
one of the best in modern cinema.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.030
It also gave Reeves the kind of showbiz juice
to make demands on future projects that other

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.640
filmmakers might not get to make.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.570
In a new interview with The New York Times,
Reeves said he was in post-production on "War

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.260
for the Planet of the Apes" when Warner Bros.
asked him to direct Affleck's version of "The

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.260
Batman."

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.170
And while Reeves told the outlet that the
iteration of the tale was, quote, "a totally

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.379
valid and cool take" on the Dark Knight, it
just wasn't the right story for him to direct.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:10.379
He said,

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.510
"I just didn't know how I would direct that."

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.989
"Whoa whoa, take it easy, sweetheart!"

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:19.620
That's right — instead of following in the
footsteps of such revered "Batman" directors

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.250
Tim Burton, Christopher Nolan, and Snyder,
Reeves put the studio on ice, declining to

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.900
shoot the script they'd sent him.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.239
He recalled to the Times:

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:32.940
"I don't think you're going to want me because
I wouldn't do this.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.959
And then they asked me what I would do.

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:39.109
[...] I said, I'm in the middle of making
this 'Apes' movie so I can't really tell you."

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:42.959
After finishing "War for the Planet of the
Apes," however, Reeves came back to Warner

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:47.260
Bros. with the one condition he needed to
direct the film: He needed to write his own

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.530
original script.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.260
The writer-director pitched his ideas for
"The Batman" and began writing the screenplay

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.069
in 2017.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:59.540
After five years and various pandemic-induced
production and release delays, "The Batman,"

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.379
starring Robert Pattinson as Batman and Zoe
Kravitz as Catwoman, opened in theaters in

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:06.129
March of 2022.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.359
It's clear from the interviews he's given
that Matt Reeves has the utmost respect for

00:03:10.359 --> 00:03:12.430
the character and mythos of Batman.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.220
He didn't want to just go in and make any
old Batman movie.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.400
He wanted to create a definitive Dark Knight
story for a new generation.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.040
As he went on to tell the Times, that involved
taking a novel approach to the Caped Crusader.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.239
"I wanted to fight against any sense that
Batman would remain static.

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.540
I wanted the stakes of the story to challenge
him in a way that shook him to his core.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.219
He has to change."

00:03:35.219 --> 00:03:40.819
To accomplish that, Reeves had to go darker
than any live-action, feature-length Batman

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:42.340
had gone before.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.049
As Reeves told TIME Magazine,

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:48.980
"I wanted to play on this idea that Batman
is going to unleash on these guys, and there's

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.390
a part of you that can't wait to see it."

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.419
But Reeves didn't want the same old fight
scenes in his film.

00:03:54.419 --> 00:03:57.889
He wanted to go bigger and darker, as he went
on to explain,

00:03:57.889 --> 00:04:00.909
"You go to a Batman movie to see that moment.

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:03.230
And then you see it's having a toxic effect.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.400
It makes you get a little unsettled."

